WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.220
I am now officially broke. She said, why don't you go get a job? Challenging and painful,

2
00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:10.100
extraordinarily scary thing to do. I am sure NVIDIA would be dead today. So he says,

3
00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:17.980
how much money do you have in your pocket? I said, $200. And for $200, I bought cash is always

4
00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:22.100
king. And where are your customers? It was one of the biggest gambles in our history.

5
00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:27.720
Be honest to yourself. You're just slowly dying. NVIDIA just took over Apple's market cap and has

6
00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:33.120
now become the second largest public company in the U S what you're about to see is a video of his

7
00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:39.580
leadership and entrepreneur class at Stanford from April, 2009. It is amazing how what he said back

8
00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:45.300
then is still consistent with what he says. Now the logic of success remains the same back then.

9
00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:49.760
He didn't have to spend that much time introducing the company's products. So he had more time to

10
00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:54.580
share his personal insights. He talked to young people about the problems they might face on their

11
00:00:54.580 --> 00:01:02.240
way to success and how to handle them. Remember to share this with friends and family who are also

12
00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:07.240
working very hard towards their goals and dreams. I'm sure this will help them in some magical ways.

13
00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:07.760
Enjoy.

14
00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:20.760
It is my extreme pleasure to introduce our guest today. We have Jensen Huang, who is the co-founder

15
00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:27.320
of NVIDIA. And I want to tell you, 18 years ago he was sitting in your seat. He was an MS student,

16
00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:31.920
a master's student here in electrical engineering. Only two years after he graduated, he founded the

17
00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:36.900
company. And he has been the president, CEO, and a member of the board of directors since its inception.

18
00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:42.420
He is also the incredibly generous donor to the School of Engineering. The new building that you see

19
00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:49.980
going up just outside is going to be named the Rank Building. And he gave $30 million to name that building.

20
00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:53.580
So hopefully 18 years from now, you guys will be doing the same.

21
00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:59.980
I gave my last pennies to Jim Plummer to build that building. I am now officially broke.

22
00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:07.420
Instead of giving you a company presentation today, what I thought I would do is just have a conversation with you.

23
00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:17.180
A lot of people talk about and write about building companies. And I can tell you firsthand that building

24
00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:26.820
a company is extraordinarily gratifying. It is also incredibly hard. And so the things that you want

25
00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:33.460
to talk about with respect to the company building process is rather expansive. You could talk about

26
00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:39.700
company building processes from a lot of different perspectives. And so I am going to try to touch on a few of them

27
00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:47.700
that I think are particularly important in my experience. So 16 years ago, NVIDIA had three people, three engineers.

28
00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:54.600
The insight that we had, some people call it vision. Vision is an awfully big word to me. Vision is an awfully big word to me

29
00:02:54.600 --> 00:03:00.460
because I believe, first of all, vision matters. Let me tell you that vision matters, and I will help you understand that in a second.

30
00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:06.700
But I like to use the word perspective because it makes it possible for anyone to have one. When you say vision,

31
00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:14.700
it feels like only a few selected visionaries of the world can have one. But everyone has a perspective. And that is in fact all vision means.

32
00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:28.700
That you see the world in a way that is either different or otherwise than somebody else. And you see opportunities that I think are, that you believe, are particularly important to go in and address.

33
00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:40.700
Our perspective at the time, this is 1993, you guys won't remember this, but the PC was Windows 3.1. CD-ROM was about to be introduced.

34
00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:50.700
There were no PCs with networks. Wireless technology, if somebody said radio, I think the word that would come to mind is FM radio.

35
00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:59.700
And so wireless technology didn't exist. The fastest microprocessor in the world was a 66 MHz, 46 DX2.

36
00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:06.700
And I don't think any of you would even use it in your tennis shoes today. And we would run our computers with that.

37
00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:12.700
And the PC was becoming used for desktop or for office automation.

38
00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:19.700
Our perspective was that this particular device was going to be unique in the sense that it has the ability to run programs.

39
00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:31.700
And what if we gave it the benefit of running 3D graphics programs so that you could explore new worlds, play games, you know, play games.

40
00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:36.700
And so we started a company and the business plan basically read something like this.

41
00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:41.700
We're going to take technology that was available only in the most expensive workstations.

42
00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:46.700
We're going to try to make it reinvent the technology and make it inexpensive.

43
00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:52.700
And the killer app was video games. And so I took this idea to Sand Hill Road.

44
00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:58.700
And they told me there was no video game market. People don't start companies to play games.

45
00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:03.700
And my parents, I remember calling my mom and telling her that I'm going to start this company.

46
00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:08.700
And she says, you know, what do you guys do? And I said, we build these things called 3D graphics chips.

47
00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:15.700
And people would use them to play games. And then she said, why don't you go get a job?

48
00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:22.700
And so now, of course, of course, games was, we believe, going to be a very large part of the marketplace.

49
00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:27.700
Now, we had that perspective for very obvious reasons. We grew up in the video game generation.

50
00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:31.700
I was the video game generation. I was the beginning of the video game generation.

51
00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:37.700
And so the entertainment value of video games, computer games, was very obvious to me.

52
00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:42.700
And I could imagine how it could be a very large market and a very large industry.

53
00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:46.700
For a lot of the people that were older, that sensibility didn't exist.

54
00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:52.700
And so notice, I just described to you a perspective about the world that we had,

55
00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:59.700
that is apparently, obviously now true, because video games is the world's largest digital media industry today.

56
00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:04.700
It is apparently true. And yet, at the time, our common sense was unique.

57
00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:13.700
Nobody would have created the technology, nobody would have created the company with the sole purpose of building technology to make video games possible.

58
00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:23.700
One of my favorite, favorite applications, this happened about, I guess about five years ago, a small company, struggling company here in Silicon Valley called Keyhole.

59
00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:30.700
They created a 3D virtual world. And it had no application. It was a 3D virtual world.

60
00:06:30.700 --> 00:06:36.700
And you start out in space, you see the earth, you zoom into any location you wanted just by typing in the address.

61
00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:41.700
I thought it was such a fabulous way of exploring the world, going to places you've never been.

62
00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:46.700
And they couldn't raise a penny. And so I was so excited about the company. We put money into the company.

63
00:06:46.700 --> 00:06:52.700
And I went everywhere and showed that demonstration. I would tell people that this is the way we're going to do search someday.

64
00:06:52.700 --> 00:06:57.700
If you want to search for something, look for an address, you would type it in, and we would fly you there.

65
00:06:57.700 --> 00:07:04.700
And satellite images will continue to download, and before you know it, you're right there on the street, and you might even see some buildings.

66
00:07:04.700 --> 00:07:10.700
That small company was eventually purchased by Google and became Google Earth.

67
00:07:10.700 --> 00:07:17.700
Google Earth is now the single largest downloaded, most frequently downloaded application in the history of mankind.

68
00:07:17.700 --> 00:07:25.700
Over 200 plus million downloads. That perspective was unique at the time and hard to sell.

69
00:07:25.700 --> 00:07:32.700
And so we had to go and explain it to venture capitalists who had to figure out whether the technology was going to be possible,

70
00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:35.700
how big was the market, because it was zero billion dollars at the time.

71
00:07:35.700 --> 00:07:43.700
So how do you extrapolate, how do you scope the size of a market when its apparent size was zero at the time?

72
00:07:43.700 --> 00:07:50.700
And you look at analyst reports, and you study market research, and all of it would say approximately zero.

73
00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:54.700
It would never show up. It's a non-category, a non-market.

74
00:07:54.700 --> 00:08:03.700
You know, it's incumbent upon the venture capitalists, and of course the founders, to try to figure out how to inspire each other into doing something together.

75
00:08:03.700 --> 00:08:07.700
And so Sequoia Capital and Sutter Hill were our venture capitalists.

76
00:08:07.700 --> 00:08:10.700
We got the company going with two million dollars.

77
00:08:10.700 --> 00:08:17.700
Many years later, Sequoia Capital came to me and said, you know, there's a couple of kids at Stanford,

78
00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:28.700
and they have this thing, and it's an internet thing, and you just type in what you're looking for, and it shows up, you know, puts up the website.

79
00:08:28.700 --> 00:08:33.700
And I said, yeah, yellow pages. I mean, you know, no duh, right?

80
00:08:33.700 --> 00:08:38.700
We use it, and there's a variety of versions of it on the web at the time.

81
00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:44.700
We use the internet, just like everybody else, to do FTP and also to visit various websites.

82
00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:47.700
And they said, should we invest in this company?

83
00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:50.700
And I said, there's no freaking way they're going to make money. That stuff is free.

84
00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:56.700
And so they said, well, you know, we can't figure it out ourselves, but it doesn't cost much to give them a million or two.

85
00:08:56.700 --> 00:09:01.700
And they invested in a small company called what eventually became Jerry's company called Yahoo.

86
00:09:01.700 --> 00:09:06.700
Notice, although I had the perspective about one thing, I didn't have the perspective about something else.

87
00:09:06.700 --> 00:09:09.700
Just because you're a visionary doesn't mean you're a visionary about everything.

88
00:09:09.700 --> 00:09:16.700
Your perspective stems from your life experiences, what's commonsensical about you, what's interesting to you.

89
00:09:16.700 --> 00:09:22.700
And so that's important to realize, that you have perspective too. Therefore, you have vision too.

90
00:09:22.700 --> 00:09:29.700
Now what's interesting about these websites, to follow on the Yahoo story, if you remember there were several other searches out there.

91
00:09:29.700 --> 00:09:36.700
Alta Vista at the time, Excite at the time, Lycos at the time, right?

92
00:09:36.700 --> 00:09:40.700
And now the question is, they're all doing search and they all did a reasonably good job.

93
00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:46.700
Now comes the question is, what was their perspective? How was their perspective different from one another?

94
00:09:46.700 --> 00:09:50.700
One website thought that they were a destination. Do you guys remember that?

95
00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:58.700
We would be a destination, kind of like a channel. Somebody said, in fact, since we're going to be a destination, we would serve up content.

96
00:09:58.700 --> 00:10:02.700
And therefore, the search part of it is a commodity. We'll outsource that.

97
00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:11.700
So all of the search engines, which started out as search, turned into destinations or portals, and they outsourced the search to someone else,

98
00:10:11.700 --> 00:10:22.700
which made it possible for Google to start. And so notice, two companies doing exactly the same thing, started with the exact same fundamental core technology,

99
00:10:22.700 --> 00:10:30.700
ended up in radically different places, because they had different perspectives. They saw the world differently. So perspective matters.

100
00:10:30.700 --> 00:10:38.700
Now in our industry, shortly after we were started, 3D graphics for PCs and consumer 3D graphics became the hottest thing.

101
00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:47.700
And so everybody in Silicon Valley was starting a 3D graphics company. We were, in 1993, the only consumer 3D graphics company in the world.

102
00:10:47.700 --> 00:10:55.700
By the end of a couple of years or so, 1995, there were probably 50, 70 startups doing exactly the same thing we were trying to do.

103
00:10:55.700 --> 00:11:03.700
Over time, we competed with about 200 companies. NVIDIA today is the only surviving computer graphics company in the world.

104
00:11:03.700 --> 00:11:07.700
And so the question is then, what happened? Competition is intense.

105
00:11:07.700 --> 00:11:16.700
Everybody has smart people. Everybody has money. We competed with IBM. We competed with HP. We competed with Silicon Graphics.

106
00:11:16.700 --> 00:11:23.700
We competed with Sony, 3D effects, S3, Sirius Logic, big, small, international, local. We competed with companies all over the world.

107
00:11:23.700 --> 00:11:34.700
So the question is, what happened? I would argue that 300 companies armed with exactly the same technology, armed with exactly the same people,

108
00:11:34.700 --> 00:11:43.700
the company that wins, and let's say they all execute. And they did. With 300 companies, you know, 50% of them were going to execute at any given point in time.

109
00:11:43.700 --> 00:11:49.700
And so the question is, why does one survive? Well, I think that it matters to have perspective. And let me give you some examples.

110
00:11:49.700 --> 00:11:58.700
I always believed that you need to understand the reason why your business works. What is the essence of your business? What makes it work?

111
00:11:58.700 --> 00:12:09.700
Now, the foundation of my business, at its core, is semiconductor technology. Here in Silicon Valley, we usually like to refer to semiconductor technology as Moore's Law.

112
00:12:09.700 --> 00:12:19.700
Moore's Law is not so much a physical law as it's a law of competition. It is a law of challenging engineers. It's a law almost of setting pace.

113
00:12:19.700 --> 00:12:32.700
And Moore's law approximately gives you twice the performance every year or two. Understanding that the fundamental ingredient of our business improves by a factor of two every year,

114
00:12:32.700 --> 00:12:39.700
and simultaneously reduces in cost by a factor of two every year. The question is, what makes a survivable business?

115
00:12:39.700 --> 00:12:54.700
And so our first perspective was that 3D graphics was insatiable. It was insatiable. That if I made something twice as good every year, even if the customer never asked for it,

116
00:12:54.700 --> 00:13:03.700
even if the customer told us it was too expensive, even if the customer, when you went to float that product specification to them, told you that they're not interested.

117
00:13:03.700 --> 00:13:10.700
And in fact, that was the case. I took our product spec to Dell and HP and IBM and Gateway, and they all told me it was too much money.

118
00:13:10.700 --> 00:13:19.700
You're well outside of the boundaries of what they're willing to pay for. When your customers all tell you not to do something, the question is then what do you do?

119
00:13:19.700 --> 00:13:31.700
In our case, because we had this unique perspective that 3D graphics was insatiable, and Moore's Law was our friend, therefore, we should make our graphics processors twice as good every year.

120
00:13:31.700 --> 00:13:37.700
And so for the first five years of our company, we just turned off our blinders and said, we're going to ignore customers.

121
00:13:37.700 --> 00:13:45.700
Now which one of you guys are going to go through your marketing courses, and the lesson that it teaches you is ignore your customers?

122
00:13:45.700 --> 00:13:51.700
Well, sometimes you have to ignore your customers. And the reason for that is because they don't know the nature of your business.

123
00:13:51.700 --> 00:13:59.700
And while the industry is being created, before there's common sense about the rules of that business, there is no way they can possibly know.

124
00:13:59.700 --> 00:14:06.700
And so we – I took the last few million dollars of the company's money and built a chip that is way, way, way too big.

125
00:14:06.700 --> 00:14:13.700
And our customers told us we were way out of bounds on cost, and they weren't going to buy any until the day we showed up with the processor.

126
00:14:13.700 --> 00:14:23.700
Innovation is a rather dangerous thing. On the one hand, once you discover a great idea, you rinse and repeat, rinse and repeat, and you make that idea better and better and better.

127
00:14:23.700 --> 00:14:30.700
Whether it's a laptop computer that you guys have here, or a car, or a microprocessor, or in our case, a graphics processor.

128
00:14:30.700 --> 00:14:35.700
We made it better and better every year. At some point, it becomes good enough.

129
00:14:35.700 --> 00:14:42.700
Moore's Law is a wonderful thing. Semiconductor technology enables you to make amazing leaps and bounds in technology.

130
00:14:42.700 --> 00:14:53.700
And at some point, it becomes good enough. And so in the – this is probably in the late 90s, about seven years into our company, maybe six, seven years into our company.

131
00:14:53.700 --> 00:15:07.700
I came to the conclusion that 3D graphics was not going to be sustainable as a accelerator or a fixed function device that renders texture maps and polygons on the screen.

132
00:15:07.700 --> 00:15:16.700
That we had to change the company to make the 3D graphics processor programmable so that it could be an artistic medium for expression.

133
00:15:16.700 --> 00:15:34.700
Now this is a weird word. We believed, unless we could figure out a way to make the content richer and more interesting and stylistically different from one game developer to another game developer to another, we would limit the life of our medium.

134
00:15:34.700 --> 00:15:43.700
And if our medium reached this end, we as the world leader would also see our end.

135
00:15:43.700 --> 00:15:58.700
Almost every single video game that you guys see today has our fingerprint on it. Whether it's an Xbox 360 or PS3 or any PC game today, you could see elements of what programmable shaders made possible.

136
00:15:58.700 --> 00:16:09.700
That started a whole new innovation curve for us and kept our industry vibrant. But the crossing from one generation of technology to the next generation almost killed the company.

137
00:16:09.700 --> 00:16:18.700
And so that process of reinventing the company, the perspective that led us to a new idea, also risks the company in the process.

138
00:16:18.700 --> 00:16:31.700
When a company gets larger, you guys are going to learn that as the founder or as the CEO, you have to learn new things. And many of the new things that you'll learn has to do with building products at first.

139
00:16:31.700 --> 00:16:45.700
And I've just talked to you about building products. Soon you'll be talking about and learning about building companies. And building companies means things that are soft and hard to explain, like building a company with a culture. What does that mean?

140
00:16:45.700 --> 00:16:53.700
How does the culture of one company different from a culture of another company? And why is it that this particular culture is better for your company and not for another?

141
00:16:53.700 --> 00:17:02.700
How do you organize? Are you functionally organized? Are you organized in business units? How do you deal with multiple products and multiple geographies and multiple customers?

142
00:17:02.700 --> 00:17:14.700
And so that's the company building process. People matter. Personalities matter. The most important thing above that is to realize that building a – when you're building a company and building a product,

143
00:17:14.700 --> 00:17:20.700
skill matters. Intellect matters. Training matters. But it's not enough.

144
00:17:20.700 --> 00:17:31.700
The part of it that is important to realize about building companies is that it's a challenging and painful and oftentimes extraordinarily scary thing to do.

145
00:17:31.700 --> 00:17:41.700
And so unless you have passion, unless you really love the process of building the company and what you're trying to do, it's going to be incredibly, incredibly challenging.

146
00:17:41.700 --> 00:17:48.700
When you're building a company, if you decide to build a company, you have to ask yourself what is the purpose that you're building the company for?

147
00:17:48.700 --> 00:17:54.700
Is it that you would like to build a company so that you can sell it, make a fortune? Is it that you would like to build a company so you can take it public?

148
00:17:54.700 --> 00:18:00.700
You're just a serial entrepreneur. You want to build something? Let somebody else run it? Build something? Sell it?

149
00:18:00.700 --> 00:18:03.700
Whatever your reason is, be honest to yourself.

150
00:18:03.700 --> 00:18:12.700
Turns out that in my case, I just love the process of building things. And I love being part of something.

151
00:18:12.700 --> 00:18:21.700
Being part of NVIDIA and being part of a people and being part of a cause that is inspiring to me keeps me vibrant.

152
00:18:21.700 --> 00:18:24.700
And it's something that I'm willing to do for a very long period of time.

153
00:18:24.700 --> 00:18:25.700
Yes, sir.

154
00:18:25.700 --> 00:18:37.700
The question is, what did our investors say about us taking the big risk of adding programmable shaders and reinventing that product category and reinventing our company?

155
00:18:37.700 --> 00:18:49.700
First of all, it's not a conversation you really have with your shareholders, but you do have the conversation with your management team first, your employees second, your board of directors third.

156
00:18:49.700 --> 00:18:52.700
Typically is the way, the process that I take.

157
00:18:52.700 --> 00:19:01.700
When you're in high tech, when you're in the technology industry, when the technology moves this fast, if you're not reinventing yourself, you're just slowly dying.

158
00:19:01.700 --> 00:19:08.700
You're just slowly dying, unfortunately, at the rate of Moore's Law, which is the fastest of any rate that we know.

159
00:19:08.700 --> 00:19:11.700
Right? The compounded rate of Moore's Law is pretty unbelievable.

160
00:19:11.700 --> 00:19:16.700
We have a product that's generating a lot of money, and it's very successful. How do you cannibalize it?

161
00:19:16.700 --> 00:19:20.700
There is a theory that if you don't cannibalize it, someone will, and it surely will be cannibalized.

162
00:19:20.700 --> 00:19:28.700
And so if you want to be a market leader, you have to take the initiative to cannibalize your own products and have your ideas cannibalize your own ideas.

163
00:19:28.700 --> 00:19:44.700
When we went from a fixed function graphics accelerator, a texture mapping engine for games like Quake 3 and Doom and those kind of games, to a programmable shading architecture, our first chip almost killed the company.

164
00:19:44.700 --> 00:19:49.700
It was called GeForce FX. I don't know if any of you have ever owned one of those.

165
00:19:49.700 --> 00:19:56.700
GeForce FX is a chip, is a processor that, you know, it's a baby only a mother can love.

166
00:19:56.700 --> 00:19:59.700
I mean, it's...

167
00:19:59.700 --> 00:20:06.700
We took enormous chance in building GeForce FX, but it almost broke our back.

168
00:20:06.700 --> 00:20:12.700
But if we didn't build that chip, I am sure NVIDIA would be dead today. I am absolutely certain we'd be dead.

169
00:20:12.700 --> 00:20:16.700
It was one of the biggest gambles in our history.

170
00:20:16.700 --> 00:20:21.700
Some people are sort of see that gaming is sort of moving more towards consoles and away from the disease.

171
00:20:21.700 --> 00:20:22.700
Yeah.

172
00:20:22.700 --> 00:20:31.700
Is it harder to make profit with, you know, when you have to negotiate with Sony or Nintendo or whoever, some big company, than it would be when you sell individual cards?

173
00:20:31.700 --> 00:20:41.700
Yeah. The economics of building anything ultimately comes down to the amount of competition you have.

174
00:20:41.700 --> 00:20:46.700
You don't set the price, the competition sets the price. The market doesn't set the price, the competition does.

175
00:20:46.700 --> 00:20:56.700
It came down to this for me. I think you have to realize what is the finite resource, what is the scarce resource that you have as a manager.

176
00:20:56.700 --> 00:21:03.700
The function of a manager is to allocate resources properly for the best return.

177
00:21:03.700 --> 00:21:17.700
And so, if you think about our resources, our resource is the finite number of extraordinary engineers and how much time they have in a day to pursue whatever opportunities that are out there.

178
00:21:17.700 --> 00:21:24.700
The number of opportunities that are out there is less than my supply of engineers. So therefore demand exceeds is less than my supply.

179
00:21:24.700 --> 00:21:36.700
Then obviously I'm very enthusiastic about it. But if it's the other way around, then the opportunity to build a game console at terrible economics, or any project at terrible economics, is simply not worth it.

180
00:21:36.700 --> 00:21:45.700
And so I look at it irrespective of competition. The competition sets the price. But then I get to decide whether I want to engage in that project or not.

181
00:21:45.700 --> 00:21:51.700
You are in charge of your own company as the CEO. And so we decide whether it's economic.

182
00:21:51.700 --> 00:21:56.700
You have to be thoughtful about what is your critical resource. Do you have more of it or less of it than the market demands?

183
00:21:56.700 --> 00:22:03.700
Do you have more opportunity or less opportunity than your resource can support? And then what's the appropriate return on that investment?

184
00:22:03.700 --> 00:22:07.700
Thinking about not just your cost, but more importantly, your opportunity cost.

185
00:22:07.700 --> 00:22:10.700
Can you tell us a little bit about the culture that you tried to set at NVIDIA?

186
00:22:10.700 --> 00:22:19.700
At the core of our company's success is innovation. Now, a lot of companies say innovation is important to their company.

187
00:22:19.700 --> 00:22:35.700
Invention is important to their company. However, I don't believe you can fundamentally say that you want as a CEO to nurture the spirit of innovation, to encourage innovation, unless you have a culture of risk-taking.

188
00:22:35.700 --> 00:22:45.700
We have to encourage our engineers, our marketing people, all of our employees to take calculated risks. First of all, you have to teach them how to do that. That's a skill.

189
00:22:45.700 --> 00:22:54.700
Then the second part of it is a matter of courage. Most people hate to fail. If you want to be successful, I would encourage you to grow a tolerance for failure.

190
00:22:54.700 --> 00:23:05.700
Now, when I mean a tolerance for failure, I don't mean, gee, what Jensen just told me is, sleep in until noon, don't do any of my homework, flunk out of all my classes, because that defines failure.

191
00:23:05.700 --> 00:23:14.700
Right? That's not what I said. What I said is, what I'm trying to say is that I want you to try things even though it is impossible to care.

192
00:23:14.700 --> 00:23:21.700
That it is impossible to calculate precisely that it would lead to success. That your instincts, your intuition is something you ought to follow.

193
00:23:21.700 --> 00:23:29.700
If it wasn't because of following my own instincts, or the founders instincts, or many of our employees instincts, why would we be where we are today?

194
00:23:29.700 --> 00:23:35.700
And why would we have invented things that the markets never had before, the world's never had before?

195
00:23:35.700 --> 00:23:47.700
So you have to have this culture or tolerance for risk taking. But the thing about failure is this. If you fail often, you actually might become a failure. And that's different than being successful.

196
00:23:47.700 --> 00:23:59.700
How do you teach someone how to fail, but fail quickly? And to change courses as soon as you know it's a dead end. And the way to do that is we call it intellectual honesty.

197
00:23:59.700 --> 00:24:07.700
We assess on a continuous basis whether something makes sense or not. And if it's the wrong decision, let's change our mind.

198
00:24:07.700 --> 00:24:14.700
And you know, a lot of people say, CEOs are always right, and they never change their mind. That doesn't make any sense at all to me.

199
00:24:14.700 --> 00:24:26.700
Especially when it violates the first principles of what we want the company to become. An innovative company that invents amazing things, that solves problems for the world that it sometimes didn't even know it had.

200
00:24:26.700 --> 00:24:37.700
If you want to do that, then you have to cultivate that tolerance for risk taking, and you have to then teach people how to fail, but fail quickly and inexpensively.

201
00:24:37.700 --> 00:24:45.700
And how to be direct with each other, that this is the wrong approach, and what's the better approach. And then, you know, be flexible enough to change courses and quick.

202
00:24:45.700 --> 00:24:56.700
And so that type of culture, if you will, in today's, you know, if you guys were to start a company and you were building a website with an internet service of some kind, internet-based service of some kind,

203
00:24:56.700 --> 00:25:10.700
with the competition coming from all over the world, and it's 24-7, and ideas take no time to experiment, a particular website or a particular company could be throwing ideas out into the world 20 a day.

204
00:25:10.700 --> 00:25:20.700
And so unless you are thoughtful about risk taking and being able to change your mind, reacting to the market conditions, and being flexible, how are you going to stay alive?

205
00:25:20.700 --> 00:25:28.700
And so you could almost see what I just described in the nature of older companies and the nature of the newer companies.

206
00:25:28.700 --> 00:25:37.700
I'm sure you guys all go to Google's website. Almost every single application is in beta form. They're trying all kinds of stuff, right? They're trying all kinds of stuff.

207
00:25:37.700 --> 00:25:44.700
If they call it production, and it doesn't work well, you guys would just be upset at them. So they call it beta. Have you noticed? They call it beta.

208
00:25:44.700 --> 00:25:50.700
So that they could try a lot of things. And if it fails, take it out. If it's bad, take it out. If it works, do more.

209
00:25:50.700 --> 00:25:57.700
Innovation requires a little bit of experimentation. Experimentation requires exploration. Exploration will result in failure.

210
00:25:57.700 --> 00:26:02.700
Unless you have a tolerance for failure, you would never experiment. And if you don't ever experiment, you would never innovate.

211
00:26:02.700 --> 00:26:05.700
If you don't innovate, you don't succeed. You would just be a dweeb.

212
00:26:05.700 --> 00:26:09.700
How did you choose your co-founders and initial teams?

213
00:26:09.700 --> 00:26:19.700
Don't ever go into business with anyone you don't deeply trust. And they were two of my closest colleagues, and I trust them completely.

214
00:26:19.700 --> 00:26:24.700
They're wonderful friends even today. As a CEO, selecting people is 99% of the job.

215
00:26:24.700 --> 00:26:33.700
Especially in the initial stage, how can you find each of your positions in the company, and how can you distribute the profit?

216
00:26:33.700 --> 00:26:45.700
Okay, so her question is, we were friends in the beginning, we're friends now. How do we figure out who's the right position, and how do we distribute the profits?

217
00:26:45.700 --> 00:26:46.700
Yeah.

218
00:26:46.700 --> 00:26:54.700
Okay. All of our pay were identical. And we all had identical share in the company. So that's, that's, that's just simply fairness.

219
00:26:54.700 --> 00:27:02.700
Now the question becomes governance. There's the part of it which is equity. Equity is another way of saying what's fair. Right?

220
00:27:02.700 --> 00:27:11.700
So we all had the same salary. All, all three of us were making $100,000 a year. Okay? And we all had a percentage of the company. Equal percentage.

221
00:27:11.700 --> 00:27:20.700
Now, you can't run a company though. You can't build a great company. When you have three people, who has to vote on everything, and with equal share of responsibilities. You simply can't.

222
00:27:20.700 --> 00:27:30.700
That becomes a leadership question. That becomes a governance question. That becomes a management question. Right? That becomes a question about building a great company.

223
00:27:30.700 --> 00:27:40.700
I don't recall exactly, exactly the conversation, but I think it kind of went like this. All right, Jensen, you're the CEO, right? Okay. That was done.

224
00:27:40.700 --> 00:27:57.700
From a personality perspective, I'm not particularly outgoing. And so that's not a necessity for being a good CEO. But as a personality, I've always been able to see around the corners, if you will.

225
00:27:57.700 --> 00:28:10.700
I can see around the fuzzy edges. And I think CEOs and leaders need to be comfortable with ambiguity. Ambiguity meaning that, you know, what does the future look like?

226
00:28:10.700 --> 00:28:20.700
Well, it's hard to say. Some people hate that. Some people just say, Jensen, tell me what you need to have done, and with how much resource, and by when.

227
00:28:20.700 --> 00:28:31.700
Some people rather, me tell them that, hey, look, there's this opportunity out there, not sure what it is, not sure how big it is, but it kind of feels like this. Let's go figure it out, and let's build a business.

228
00:28:31.700 --> 00:28:48.700
Some people are very comfortable with that. And so this ambiguity is important to be comfortable with, I guess. And I think that all CEOs that are very successful are comfortable with ambiguity. And I am very comfortable with ambiguity. Yes?

229
00:28:48.700 --> 00:28:49.700
Yes.

230
00:28:49.700 --> 00:29:01.700
Yeah. What percentage of your initial investment was yours? Like, how do you get the rest? And also, like, how many times was your proposal for help with the investment?

231
00:29:01.700 --> 00:29:30.700
Mm-hmm. So I was 30 years old, and I had never taken a single business class, and I've never taken any marketing classes. And I've never used a – at the time, it wasn't PowerPoint, it was called Persuasion. On the Mac, it was called Persuasion. And so I bought a Mac so I could use Persuasion. And then I tried to create a company presentation to take it to venture capitalists.

232
00:29:30.700 --> 00:29:45.700
The process kind of went like this. We started – my first official day of work was my 30th birthday, February 17th. We got the company funded. And so once we got started, the question is, what are we going to do? You know, how does it all work out? How do we start the company?

234
00:30:15.700 --> 00:30:23.700
You know, what are we going to do today? Or, you know, Chinese food tomorrow or whatever? That would be like a big deal. And then after a while, it was like, could you put some donuts in the fridge in the morning for when we come?

235
00:30:23.700 --> 00:30:31.700
I mean, so that would be a big deal for a while. And so that lasted for a few months, just the three of us like that. I know it sounds pathetic, but it's true.

236
00:30:31.700 --> 00:30:44.700
Because at that time, I'm reading about books on how to start companies, and I'm trying to figure out, you know, how to go raise money, and, you know, what's a venture capitalist, and how do you incorporate the company, and those kind of things.

237
00:30:44.700 --> 00:31:02.700
Pretty soon, I met a lawyer, went to a law firm called Cooley Godward, and they helped us incorporate the company. The amount of money that he says, you know, we need some money from you so that we could price the shares, and also to incorporate the company.

238
00:31:02.700 --> 00:31:15.700
So he says, how much money do you have in your pocket? I said, $200. So he says, okay, give me $200. I gave him $200. And for $200, I bought 15%, I think it was 20% of NVIDIA.

239
00:31:22.700 --> 00:31:32.700
So it was a good deal. Yeah, 20%, yeah. And then I went back to the house, and then went back to the condo, and they said,

240
00:31:32.700 --> 00:31:37.700
they both gave me $200, and they both got 20%. And that's how it worked. Literally.

241
00:31:37.700 --> 00:31:41.700
Here's the thing. NVIDIA, I never finished my business plan.

242
00:31:41.700 --> 00:31:48.700
I know it. I know it. We never finished a business plan.

243
00:31:48.700 --> 00:31:52.700
Never could figure out how to finish a business plan, to tell you the truth.

244
00:31:52.700 --> 00:32:01.700
If I were to finish that book, and I went to Borders and got Gordon Bell's book, How to Start a High-Tech Company.

245
00:32:01.700 --> 00:32:05.700
It's like this thick. If I would have read the whole thing, I would have been dead now.

246
00:32:06.700 --> 00:32:13.700
We would have run out of money, ran out of time. And so I read the first three or four chapters, and I, you know, I got to go to work.

247
00:32:13.700 --> 00:32:19.700
And so I incorporated the company. They introduced us to two venture capitalists.

248
00:32:19.700 --> 00:32:29.700
And I just went to their office and told them what I like to do. The thing that gets the company funded, and when you're, when you get to that point, you just have to remember a few things.

249
00:32:29.700 --> 00:32:42.700
VCs don't invest in business plans, because business plans are easy to write. I couldn't write it, but other people could. And so, so they invest in this. They invest in great people.

250
00:32:42.700 --> 00:33:02.700
And so the, so the question is, is do they trust you? Your reputation matters. Your history matters. Because, because I had done so much work with Andy Bechtelsheim, which was another graduate of Stanford, of Stanford, and the founder of Sun, and worked with the founders of Synopsys and LSI Logic.

251
00:33:02.700 --> 00:33:15.700
And, you know, we were all very successful, and we did good work. Your reputation will precede you, even if your business plan writing skills are, you know, inadequate.

252
00:33:15.700 --> 00:33:23.700
And the second thing is, you need to have a vision that's sufficiently large to invest in. Because their statistics, their probability of success is rather low.

253
00:33:23.700 --> 00:33:32.700
And if they need to put in $10 million, if the market's only $20 million large, they'll never get that $10 million back with reasonable return.

254
00:33:32.700 --> 00:33:45.700
But if it's a $200 billion market, then of course it's a rather different thing. Okay, so the size of the market. And they want to know that, that at least there is a clever idea that the market has never, never done before.

255
00:33:45.700 --> 00:33:53.700
So that last part is probably second, you know, last. I said it last, because also I think it's the least important.

256
00:33:53.700 --> 00:34:00.700
You might have to reinvent yourself over time. And if you want to reinvent yourself, you need to have great people. That's why great people is so important. Yes, sir?

257
00:34:00.700 --> 00:34:07.700
Who were some of the people that you considered to be your mentors when you were getting started? And what was some of the best advice that you got from them?

258
00:34:07.700 --> 00:34:14.700
I truly believe that if you want to be successful, a successful habit is to have the capacity and the willingness to learn from just about anybody.

259
00:34:14.700 --> 00:34:24.700
You know, I learned from just about anybody. And it could be a little thing, it could be a big thing. You know, if it wasn't because of my kids, I would be, I would, I would, I would miss the whole internet age.

260
00:34:24.700 --> 00:34:37.700
You know, I would have missed YouTube and Facebook and Twitter and, you know, I mean, without, so you need to, you need to know that, that the world changes and you want to be able to learn from just about anybody.

261
00:34:37.700 --> 00:34:49.700
And so I, I'm surrounded with extraordinarily talented executives and professionals of all walks of life. And so you just have to make sure that you're, you're willing to learn from just about anybody.

262
00:34:49.700 --> 00:35:00.700
Some of the, some of the great advice that I've, I've had over the years, focus, laser beam focus. You know, don't do too much, do, do a few things well.

263
00:35:00.700 --> 00:35:11.700
And do it with extraordinary intensity. You know, focus, focus matters. If you look at what I do with my time, I wake up in the morning and the first thing in my time is NVIDIA and the last thing I do is NVIDIA.

264
00:35:11.700 --> 00:35:16.700
And I do that 24-7. And if I could figure out a way to do that for another 50 years, we're going to be a good company.

265
00:35:16.700 --> 00:35:27.700
So, um, uh, her question is, um, she started out by saying that, that I was a successful, I'm a successful entrepreneur and, and, uh, uh, and what are my biggest challenges now?

266
00:35:27.700 --> 00:35:34.700
And, um, considering that I like ambiguity, what's my best estimate of the future for the company or, and, and for myself?

267
00:35:34.700 --> 00:35:39.700
The biggest challenge with building a company is the reinvention of the company.

268
00:35:39.700 --> 00:35:45.700
Every successful thing needs to be torn down at some point and be rebuilt.

269
00:35:45.700 --> 00:35:52.700
It is unfortunate but true. And the reason for that is because the technology either gets good enough and therefore you have to reinvent.

270
00:35:52.700 --> 00:35:58.700
And sometimes the invention process is disruptive. Sometimes it's in fact destructive.

271
00:35:58.700 --> 00:36:02.700
And it could be, it could destroy, you could destroy what you have built in the past.

272
00:36:02.700 --> 00:36:11.700
And so the reinvention process is very challenging. It's gut-wrenching. It takes a lot of courage. And, um, it really tests your conviction.

273
00:36:11.700 --> 00:36:17.700
In the technology industry, reinventing the company every 10 years is almost a necessary thing.

274
00:36:17.700 --> 00:36:23.700
So that's, when I say challenging, I don't mean bad challenging. I think that's fun challenging.

275
00:36:23.700 --> 00:36:26.700
I love the process of reinvention.

276
00:36:26.700 --> 00:36:35.700
Now, what's my best forecast for our company? I think that NVIDIA has the opportunity to become one of the most important technology companies in the world.

277
00:36:35.700 --> 00:36:47.700
I hope that, I hope that it does. And my best forecast for me is that, um, I am 80 years old and I'm here talking to students and I hope I'm still the CEO.

278
00:36:47.700 --> 00:36:52.700
So the gentleman's question is, has to do with, uh, in the beginning, uh, survival is important, cash is king.

279
00:36:52.700 --> 00:36:59.700
Just so that there's no, no, no ambiguity about this. Survival is always important. Cash is always king.

280
00:36:59.700 --> 00:37:03.700
And so as the CEO, you're either making money, saving money or raising money.

281
00:37:03.700 --> 00:37:10.700
And, and if you're not making money, raising money and saving money, you ought to be doing those three things.

282
00:37:10.700 --> 00:37:16.700
It's a simple, it's a, just stay focused on those three things. And so when you're, during the beginning, in the early years,

283
00:37:16.700 --> 00:37:21.700
I was raising money all the time. As soon as I was done raising this round of money, I gotta raise more money.

284
00:37:21.700 --> 00:37:27.700
You know, you're always raising money. Just, maybe, maybe there was a week break in between, but I was raising money all the time.

285
00:37:27.700 --> 00:37:32.700
I was, as a startup, you're always going out of business. Right? That's the definition of a startup.

286
00:37:32.700 --> 00:37:35.700
An enterprise that is nearly out of business all the time.

287
00:37:35.700 --> 00:37:39.700
Yes, ma'am.

288
00:37:39.700 --> 00:37:43.700
Are you prepared for the leadership succession for a company?

289
00:37:43.700 --> 00:37:48.700
Well, I wanted this job until I'm 80, I just said. No, I'm just kidding.

290
00:37:48.700 --> 00:37:56.700
One of the primary roles of a CEO, in order to grow the company, in order to make, make NVIDIA one of the most important technology companies in the world,

291
00:37:56.700 --> 00:38:03.700
and make, make significant contribution to society, in order to do that, you have to cultivate new leaders.

292
00:38:03.700 --> 00:38:10.700
So that they can have new ideas and grow new businesses, and, and, you know, maybe run a different geography, run a new, new different product line.

293
00:38:10.700 --> 00:38:18.700
And so, I spend most of my time these days, most of my time these days, sitting with our general managers and sitting with our leaders,

294
00:38:18.700 --> 00:38:25.700
and helping them think through strategies, and helping them think through challenges, and helping them think through product roadmaps,

295
00:38:25.700 --> 00:38:34.700
and helping them think through transitions, and, you know, team building, organization creation, you know, how to manage, how to, how to create processes that, that last the test of time.

296
00:38:34.700 --> 00:38:42.700
So, so, you know, these things are, are lessons that, that I'm supposed to pass on, and I do. And I spend a lot of my time doing that.

297
00:38:42.700 --> 00:38:55.700
That succession planning, by a priori, picking out three people that the board should consider, in the case that I get run over by a bus, is a toxic, toxic process.

298
00:38:55.700 --> 00:39:03.700
I know that it has been, it has been thought of as a, the methodology, a succession planning, but I think it's just very toxic for the environment,

299
00:39:03.700 --> 00:39:13.700
because everybody's trying to figure out who got selected and who didn't. I think that it's a much, much better process to focus on ultimately developing the next generation of leaders,

300
00:39:13.700 --> 00:39:21.700
so that in the case that something happens, or I'm not the right CEO anymore, there are many choices for the board to choose from, including outside.

301
00:39:21.700 --> 00:39:24.700
Hi. I don't know if it's a good question, but...

302
00:39:24.700 --> 00:39:25.700
It's okay.

303
00:39:25.700 --> 00:39:28.700
What do you think that is a problem with Latin America?

304
00:39:28.700 --> 00:39:31.700
Any question from, from our Latino crowd here is fabulous.

305
00:39:31.700 --> 00:39:33.700
I am a little.

306
00:39:33.700 --> 00:39:39.700
Let me see if I can paraphrase the question. You know, you know friends who have a lot of ideas, but they also have a lot of money,

307
00:39:39.700 --> 00:39:43.700
and so they don't really feel that motivated to do something with those ideas.

308
00:39:43.700 --> 00:39:53.700
You know, first of all, money, I hope that if I leave you with anything, money is the only singular reason not to start the company.

309
00:39:53.700 --> 00:40:03.700
Because starting companies are, it's a very, very unlikely probability for success. If that is your reason for doing it, you will likely regret the experience.

310
00:40:03.700 --> 00:40:13.700
You should start the company and build the company, because you just, you so believe in your idea, you're so passionate about it, and you want to build something great.

311
00:40:13.700 --> 00:40:17.700
The only reason you want to do it. It just comes along. It's fun.

312
00:40:17.700 --> 00:40:27.700
I have, I have plenty of money, and, and, and, and, but, but it doesn't motivate me, nor does it demotivate.

313
00:40:27.700 --> 00:40:34.700
I would urge you to ask yourself, and for your friends to ask, ask themselves, what is the purpose that they want to start a company?

314
00:40:34.700 --> 00:40:40.700
Ideas are a dime a dozen. There's so many ideas. If you want ideas, you're going to get a lot of ideas in this room.

315
00:40:40.700 --> 00:40:52.700
And so ideas don't really matter. You have to have a perspective that's unique, that you feel really strongly about it, that you're willing to persevere almost any challenge.

316
00:40:52.700 --> 00:40:54.700
Okay? Thank you, everybody.
